{
    "title": "Continuation of the National Emergency With Respect to the Situation in Nicaragua",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "November 24, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-24/pdf/2025-21030.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has extended for one more year the national emergency related to the situation in Nicaragua, first declared in 2018. The notice says Nicaragua\u2019s government continues to undermine democratic institutions, use repression against civilians, and engage in corruption, which the United States views as a threat to its national security and foreign policy.\n\nThis move keeps existing U.S. tools in place, such as targeted restrictions tied to the emergency. It may be welcomed by those who want continued pressure on Nicaragua\u2019s leaders, including human rights advocates and members of the Nicaraguan diaspora who seek accountability. It also provides clarity for U.S. officials and companies that have been operating under these rules, helping them plan for the year ahead.\n\nAt the same time, extending the emergency can carry costs. Businesses with ties to Nicaragua may face ongoing compliance burdens and limited access to partners. Trade and investment can be harder, which can ripple through local economies. Ordinary Nicaraguans, who are not the intended targets, may feel indirect effects if the economy slows or if financial channels tighten. Humanitarian groups may also need to navigate more paperwork to deliver aid, even when exemptions exist.\n\nIn short, the United States is keeping its current approach in place for another year, signaling continued concern while maintaining pressure. The practical impact will depend on how the measures are enforced, whether there are pathways for humanitarian relief and civic support, and whether conditions in Nicaragua change in the months ahead.",
